### Describe the bug

## Bug description

Mouse drag interaction on `AngleSlider` is broken since v9.1.0. The drag stops immediately after the mouse moves, making the component uncontrollable by mouse. It worked correctly in v9.0.2.

## Reproduction steps

1. Render an `AngleSlider` component
2. Click and hold the slider knob
3. Try to drag the mouse

**Expected:** The slider value updates as the mouse moves while held down.  
**Actual:** The drag stops immediately; value does not follow the mouse.

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/711f98b9-52dd-4b93-87b7-e5cacdc22178

## Root cause

The regression was introduced by commit `93c6beb` ("Fix dead code in use-radial move", Apr 9, 2026), which is included in v9.1.0 but not in v9.0.2.

The commit added `document.removeEventListener` calls to the **refCallback cleanup function** in `use-radial-move.ts`:

```ts
// v9.1.0 refCallback cleanup (buggy)
return () => {
  if (node) {
    node.removeEventListener('mousedown', onMouseDown);
    node.removeEventListener('touchstart', handleTouchStart);
  }
  // ← These lines were added in 93c6beb
  document.removeEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove, false);
  document.removeEventListener('mouseup', handleMouseUp, false);
  document.removeEventListener('touchmove', handleTouchMove, false);
  document.removeEventListener('touchend', handleTouchEnd, false);
};
```

Because `refCallback` is created with `useCallback([onChange])`, it is **recreated every time `onChange` changes** (which happens on each drag step since `onChange` calls `setState` in the parent). In React 19, when a ref callback is recreated, the previous cleanup function is called immediately before the new callback runs.

This means:
1. User starts dragging → `beginTracking()` adds `mousemove`/`mouseup` listeners to `document`
2. `onChange(newValue)` triggers a re-render in the parent
3. The parent's new `onChange` reference causes `refCallback` to be recreated
4. React 19 calls the **old cleanup**, which removes the `document` listeners
5. Drag tracking is destroyed mid-drag

In v9.0.2 the cleanup did **not** remove `document` listeners, so this race condition did not occur.

## Suggested fix

Remove the `document.removeEventListener` calls from the refCallback cleanup, or restructure the hook so that `document` listeners are managed independently of the ref lifecycle.

```ts
// Suggested fix
return () => {
  if (node) {
    node.removeEventListener('mousedown', onMouseDown);
    node.removeEventListener('touchstart', handleTouchStart);
  }
  // Do NOT remove document listeners here
};
```

## Version info

| | Version |
|---|---|
| Mantine | 9.1.0 (broken), 9.0.2 (works) |
| Component | `AngleSlider` (uses `use-radial-move` hook internally) |

